# scip-families.dl6 -- BOTH EXTRACTOR FAMILIES AS ORDINARY HOSTS.
#
# One demand row per family, each landing EDB arrivals through the served
# engine. Nothing here is a construct this arc invented: two `sh` declarations,
# two demand rels, four level rules. The extraction-fork verdict said the
# extraction planes take the HOST shape and feed rules as ordinary deltas, and
# this is that sentence as a program.
#
# THE PROGRAM ALSO CARRIES THE DISCRIMINATION. Its corpus is the alpha/beta/
# gamma trio: alpha and beta each export a function called `helper`, and gamma
# imports alpha's and calls it. Read the three rels the program derives:
#
#   scip_def    both helpers, because both ARE defined
#   scip_ref    gamma referencing ALPHA's helper, because the compiler resolved
#               the import
#   diet_edge   EMPTY across those files, because two corpus definitions share
#               the name and a name match has no basis to choose
#
# That is the whole reason the two families carry different names, expressed in
# rows rather than in prose.
#
# Its rx lowering, per the "every snippet carries its lowering" law. Both hosts
# are the shipped demand shape (RX-H2), identical but for the command:
#
#   demand$.pipe(
#     groupBy(row => row.witnessDigest),          // content-addressed identity
#     mergeMap(g => g.pipe(take(1), mergeMap(runFamily))),
#     mergeMap(commitEdbArrival))
#
#   scipDef$ = arrivals$.pipe(filter(r => r.record === 'scip_def'),
#                             map(({ symbol, file }) => ({ symbol, file })))
#
# The `record` filter is a plain `filter`, which is exactly what the level rules
# below spell as a comparison.

# ── the two hosts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `$DL_EXTRACT_BIN` and `$SCIP_CACHE` are ENVIRONMENT references expanded by the
# child's own shell, so this program text names no absolute path, no build
# profile, and no cache location. The receipt script resolves all of them.
#
# `record` is a declared output column because it is the wire's own
# discriminator and the projection needs it: `--family scip` interleaves several
# record shapes, and scip_def and scip_ref BOTH carry a symbol and a file. The
# named projection alone cannot tell them apart, so the rules below filter on
# the tag the wire already puts on every line.
rel scip_rel(root: text) -> (record: text, symbol: text, file: text).

# The diet family's universe IS its argument list (it resolves across exactly
# the files it is handed), so the template globs the root. One record shape per
# declaration, and `resolved_edge` is the one with both paths.
rel diet_rel(root: text) -> (record: text, caller_path: text, callee_path: text).

# ── one demand row per family ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two rels rather than one shared rel, so each family's demand is separately
# assertable and either can be driven without the other.
rel want_scip(root: text).
rel want_diet(root: text).

# ── the real-SCIP plane ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
rel scip_def(symbol: text, file: text).
scip_def(symbol, file) <-
  want_scip(root), scip_rel(root, record, symbol, file), record == 'scip_def'.

rel scip_ref(symbol: text, file: text).
scip_ref(symbol, file) <-
  want_scip(root), scip_rel(root, record, symbol, file), record == 'scip_ref'.

# ── the diet plane ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
rel diet_edge(caller_path: text, callee_path: text).
diet_edge(caller_path, callee_path) <-
  want_diet(root),
  diet_rel(root, record, caller_path, callee_path),
  record == 'resolved_edge'.
